[signal arg_type="dict" arg="{"comic_name":"intro_comic","purpose":"start"}"]
set {comic_complete} = false
label keeploading
[wait time="0.2"]
if {comic_complete} == false:
	if {page} == 0.0:
		if {panel} == 5.0:
			Narrator: [A woman's voice breaks through the static.]
			[signal arg_type="dict" arg="{"purpose":"next_panel"}"]
			jump keeploading
	elif {page} == 1.0:
		if {panel} == 0.0:
			Rose: -llo?
			[signal arg_type="dict" arg="{"purpose":"next_panel"}"]
			jump keeploading
		elif {panel} == 2.0:
			Rose: Helloooo~?
			[signal arg_type="dict" arg="{"purpose":"next_panel"}"]
			jump keeploading
	[wait_input]
	[signal arg_type="dict" arg="{"purpose":"next_panel"}"]
	jump keeploading
Jessie: H-hello? Is someone there?!
Narrator: [A woman's voice breaks through the static.]
Rose: -llo?
Rose: Helloooo~?
Core/Jessie: H-hello? Is someone there?!
Rose: Hello? Oh my god, it worked? It actually worked?! Thank God!
Core/Jessie: W-who is this? How did you…the radio tower, I thought it was down?
Rose: I fixed it!
Core/Jessie: Fixed? How even… n-never mind that. You… you’re in the city then?
Rose: Well, yeah. Honestly, the radio was my last hope of really… finding anyone. I’m glad it worked.
Core/Jessie: Well, I’m also glad it worked. Frankly, my situation is dire over here. I’m running low on everything, and I’ve long since raided whatever was left of the nearby stores. I’m worried about going into the city for supplies.
Rose: Yeah…it’s pretty bad. The streets are overrun, and in the chaos of the outbreak, half the city is gridlocked with abandoned cards and the military’s poor attempt to control and quarantine…
Core/Jessie: ...
Rose: Sorry, didn’t mean to make things worse…
Core/Jessie: It’s alright. I’m just at a bit of a loss, honestly. Don’t get me wrong, being able to talk to you-- to anyone-- is a big improvement. But it doesn’t change things.
Rose: Right. I suppose not…
Core/Jessie: Sorry. Didn’t mean to shut you down like that. I’m thankful, I just... wish it were different.
Narrator: [Rose scoffs.]
Rose: Don't we all?
Core/Jessie: I suppose you're right.
Narrator: Jessie chuckles.
Rose: So… I never asked your name.
Core/Jessie: Ah, right. My name’s Jessie.
Rose: Jessie… that’s a nice name. I’m Rose.
Rose: And yes, like the lady from the Titanic.
Core/Jessie: I’d make a joke about the tragedy, but uh… feels like that would hit too close to home.
Rose: I don’t mind. Really, telling shitty jokes to myself is the only thing that’s kept me going. I’d rather tell shitty jokes to someone else.
Core/Jessie: Well, I’m all ears.
Rose: You know…
Narrator: [Silence crackles on the other end for a moment.]
Core/Jessie: Hello? Rose?
Rose: S-sorry, I was just thinking. It was nothing…
Core/Jessie: Doesn’t sound like nothing.
Rose: Well… I know this is abrupt, and we met literally less than 5 minutes ago, but… we could try to meet up. I know I said the streets are gridlocked and overrun, but I know these streets like the back of my hand. I have no doubt I could guide you through the city safely.
Rose: Besides, those things are slower at night. If you travel while the sun’s down, I’m sure it would be fine…
Narrator: [Rose pauses for a moment before jumping in again.]
Rose: A-ah, I’m getting ahead of myself. Sorry…
Core/Jessie: No, no it’s fine. I… I would like that. Don’t get me wrong, even just the radio is enough to keep me going a little while longer now, but…it would make the end of the world a whole lot less lonely if I had someone to share it with.
Rose: Right?!
Narrator: [Rose clears her throat in embarrassment.]
Rose: A-anyway. Are you sure? I was sort of just throwing it out there. I mean, don’t get me wrong, I’d do anything to talk to a real person rather than all the plants around my place. It's just that… you know. It’s dangerous making that journey.
Core/Jessie: Ma’am you’re talking to a certified firefighter. I’ve had my fair share of danger, what’s a little more?
Rose: A firefighter, huh? Impressive…
Core/Jessie: Isn't it?
Rose: Alright, don’t go getting a big head about it now.
Narrator: [Rose takes a deep breath and loudly exhales before speaking again.]
Rose: Look, if you’re going to make this journey, you better get prepared. And I don’t mean packing up everything you could ever possibly carry-- I mean the opposite. You need to travel light. The faster you can move, the better.
Rose: There’s no use in fighting these things, hacking them apart only slows them down. It doesn’t stop them. You’re better off avoiding them entirely if you can.
Core/Jessie: How do you know so much about these things?
Rose: Let’s just say it wasn’t a walk in the park repairing that repeater.
Core/Jessie: Alright, you’re the pro. I trust you.
Rose: Already? Didn’t your mom ever tell you not to trust strangers?
Core/Jessie: This isn’t a James Bond film. Sorry to disappoint, but I generally have faith in humanity.
Rose: You shouldn't.
Core/Jessie: What?
Rose: Anyway, if you’re mostly moving under the cover of night, that means it’ll take a few days to get here. You’ll have to camp out in some places, so be sure to bring something you can use to defend yourself during the night. But nothing more. Remember\: fighting is a last resort.
Core/Jessie: Got it.
Rose: I’ll let you get ready. We can talk about the next steps once you’re done.
Core/Jessie: Roger that.
Rose: ...
Core/Jessie: (Aside) Why did I say that…
set {opening_finished} = true
[signal arg="nextpage"]